'''Narglatches''' were a [[Sentience|non-sentient]] [[feline]] [[species]] of [[Diet|carnivorous]] [[mammal]]ian [[cat]]s [[Homeworld|native]] to the [[planet]]s of [[Orto Plutonia]], [[Onderon]] and [[Naboo]]. These [[feline]]s were considered sacred by Orto Plutonia's indigenous [[Talz]], who used them as mounts and for protection and a source of food.

==Biology and appearance==
Narglatches were a [[Sentience|non-sentient]] [[feline]] [[species]] of large quadrupedal [[Diet|carnivorous]]<ref name="bestiary" /> [[mammal]]ian<ref name="VE" /> [[cat]]s.<ref name="Talz-DB">{{Databank|talz|Talz}}</ref> They were viscous stealth predators<ref name="DB" /> with sharp claws and fangs.<ref name="bestiary" /> They were 6 [[meter]]s (20 [[Foot|feet]])<ref name="Ultimate" /> to 6.2 meters long and had a coat of brilliant blue fur,<ref name="bestiary" /> a mane of fleshy<ref name="DD">''[[Dark Disciple]]''</ref> quill-like spines, and a tail with a fin. Their four powerful limbs made them fast runners and in combination with their [[knife]]-like [[Tooth|teeth]] and claws, they were deadly hunters.<ref name="TCWS1-Bestiary">{{SW|url=news/monsters-of-the-clone-wars-a-season-one-bestiary|text=Monsters of ''The Clone Wars'': A Season One Bestiary|int=Monsters of The Clone Wars|nolive=1}}</ref> The narglatches were adaptable to various climates and were native to the [[planet]]s of [[Orto Plutonia]],<ref name="DB" /> [[Onderon]] and [[Naboo]].<ref name="bestiary" /> Their blue pelt helped them blend into the snowy environments of Orto Plutonia.<ref name="DB" />

Male and female narglatches only gathered in pairs to [[Reproduction|mate]]. While they were fiercer hunters than them, female narglatches were easily chased away from their slain prey by opportunistic males.<ref name="AEYNtK">''[[Star Wars: Absolutely Everything You Need to Know]]''</ref>

==Narglatches in the galaxy==
[[File:Thi-Sen_narglatch.png|thumb|left|200px|Talz chieftain [[Thi-Sen]] riding a narglatch]]
The sentient [[Talz]] species indigenous to Orto Plutonia considered the narglatches to be sacred<ref name="TCWS1-Bestiary" /><ref name="AlienArchive" /> and used them as mounts and for protection.<ref name="DB" /> The Talz also hunted them for their meat,<ref name="AlienArchive" /> which could be the base for a [[broth]] eaten by the Talz,<ref name="TCWS1-Bestiary" /> and their skins they used for clothing, and their bones which they used to construct tools and their shelters.<ref name="AEYNtK" /> In [[21 BBY]],<ref name="Trespass-Date">{{TCWcanondates|115}}</ref> the Talz used the narglatches as mounts as part of [[skirmish on Orto Plutonia|their skirmish]] with the [[Pantoran]]s of Orto Plutonia's [[moon]] [[Pantora]] and the [[clone trooper]]s of the [[501st Legion]] of the [[Galactic Republic]].<ref name="Trespass" />

During the Battles of [[Battle of Umbara|Umbara]], [[Invasion of Scipio|Scipio]], and [[Battle of Anaxes|Anaxes]], a [[Low Altitude Assault Transport/infantry|Republic gunship]] with a narglatch painted on the nose was present.<ref name="Darkness on Umbara">{{TCW|Darkness on Umbara}}</ref> By [[19 BBY]],<ref name="DD-Date">{{TCWcanondates|DD}}</ref> an enormous statue<ref name="DD" /> of the Pantoran [[Baron]] [[Notluwiski Papanoida]]<ref name="UntoldTCW">{{SWYouTube|7yKM6RExLlI|The Untold Clone Wars Panel &#124; Star Wars Celebration Anaheim}}</ref> was placed on top of a pillar surrounded by stone statues of narglatches in the center of the [[main square]] of [[Pantoran City]], which the monument dominated. That year, the [[bounty hunter]] [[Asajj Ventress]] looked at the statue during [[Capture of Moregi|her pursuit]] of her [[bounty]] [[Moregi]] and jumped on the back of one of the narglatches before jumping to an [[Zardossa airspeeder|airspeeder]].<ref name="DD" />

Narglatches were sometimes hunted for their pelt, which was vulnerable to [[blaster]] fire and considered to be valuable trophies by some cultures such as the [[Trandoshan]]s.<ref name="Ultimate" /> At least one narglatch was hunted and killed by [[Garnac's hunting guild]] and its pelt was later mounted in their [[Ubrikkian Floating Fortress]] at the time of the Clone Wars.<ref name="Padawan Lost">{{TCW|Padawan Lost}}</ref> In [[18 BBY]],<ref name="AiWS-Date">''[[Adventures in Wild Space: The Nest]]'' takes place in between ''[[Adventures in Wild Space: The Escape]]'' and ''[[Adventures in Wild Space: The Steal]]'', which ''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]'' date to [[18 BBY]].</ref> a dead narglatch was kept in a constant attack pose as part of the trophies in the [[Xirl hunting lodge|hunting lodge]] of the [[human]] big game hunter [[Gozetta]] on the [[Wild Space]] planet [[Xirl]].<ref name="AWIS3">''[[Adventures in Wild Space: The Nest]]''</ref>

Narglatch cubs were also considered to be cute and were often sold as pets<ref name="Ultimate" /> or to traveling carnivals, but as the animals matured, their temperament diminished<ref name="bestiary" /> and their ferocity increased<ref name="AlienArchive" /> and they became extremely dangerous<ref name="Ultimate" /> in confined spaces.<ref name="AlienArchive" /> As such, they were often released from captivity by their owners when they were close to adolescence<ref name="bestiary" /> or escaped.<ref name="Ultimate" /> Feral narglatches were considered to be a threat on some planets<ref name="bestiary" /> such as [[Coruscant]].<ref name="Ultimate" /> The [[Dug]] [[Preigo]] owned a friendly narglatch,<ref name="TCWS5-Bestiary">{{SW|url=news/monsters-of-the-clone-wars-a-season-five-bestiary|text=Monsters of ''The Clone Wars'': A Season Five Bestiary|int=Monsters of The Clone Wars|nolive=1}}</ref> which was one of several performers in the Dug's traveling [[circus]] on [[Florrum]], called [[Preigo's Traveling World of Wonder]] during the Clone Wars. During his brief time at the circus, the [[youngling]] [[Wookiee]] [[Gungi]] was also dressed like a narglatch.<ref name="Bound for Rescue">{{TCW|Bound for Rescue}}</ref>

In [[29 ABY]],<ref name="Perfect-Date">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]'' dates the events of "[[The Perfect Weapon]]" to [[29 ABY]].</ref> the [[mercenary]] [[Bazine Netal]] was hired for a job on the planet [[Vashka]] and was warned that she would have a competitor for the job using the code name "Narglatch". Later during her mission she unwillingly worked with the Pantoran [[Orri Tenro]], which she suspected of possibly being "Narglatch" due to the connection between Tento's species and the creatures. "Narglatch" later revealed himself to be her former mentor [[Delphi Kloda]], who had also anonymously hired for the job, and explained that he used the code name because he considered himself to be a solitary hunter. After trapping Netal, Kloda tried to escape riding a narglatch, but Netal caught up to them and fought and killed them both.<ref name="TPW">"[[The Perfect Weapon]]"</ref> In [[34 ABY]],<ref name="TFA-Date">{{FilmDate|VII}}</ref> the [["Huttsplitter" blaster rifle]] of [[Tasu Leech]], the leader of the [[Kanjiklub]] criminal gang, was adorned with a Narglatch tusk [[vibro-spike]] as a [[bayonet]].<ref name="TFAVD">''[[Star Wars: The Force Awakens: The Visual Dictionary]]''</ref> Delphi Kloda had also additionally once been Leech's number decades prior.<ref name="TPW" />

==Behind the scenes==
The narglatches were designed by concept artist [[Terryl Whitlatch]] as a possible creature of Naboo<ref name="DB old">{{DB|creatures|narglatch|narglatch}}</ref> for the [[1999]] film ''[[Star Wars: Episode I The Phantom Menace]]''.<ref name="TCW Bestiary">{{SW|url=news/monsters-of-the-clone-wars-a-season-five-bestiary|text=Monsters of ''The Clone Wars'': A Season Five Bestiary|int=Monsters of The Clone Wars|nolive=1}}</ref> [[Narglatch/Legends|The creature]] then first appeared in the 1999 [[Star Wars Legends|Legends]] [[Star Wars video games|video game]] ''[[Star Wars Episode I: The Gungan Frontier|The Gungan Frontier]]''<ref name="Star Wars Episode I: The Gungan Frontier">[[Star Wars Episode I: The Gungan Frontier|''Star Wars'' Episode I: ''The Gungan Frontier'']]</ref> and was expanded upon in the [[2001]] book ''[[The Wildlife of Star Wars: A Field Guide]]'' by Whitlash and [[Bob Carrau]].<ref name="Wildlife">''[[The Wildlife of Star Wars: A Field Guide]]''</ref>

[[File:PapanoidaStatueModel-TCW.png|thumb|The model of the statues in the Pantoran City main square]]
The Narglatches then appeared in in "[[Trespass]]," the fifteenth episode of [[The Clone Wars: Season One|Season One]] of the animated series ''[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars]]'',<ref name="Trespass" /> released on [[January 30]], [[2009]],<ref name="Trespass EG old">{{SWArchive|url=theclonewars/guide/episode015.html|text=''The Clone Wars'' Episode Guide: Trespass}}</ref> which marked their first appearance in the current [[canon]].<ref name="Trespass" /> Concept art of the creature for the episode was done by [[Wayne Lo]].<ref name="Art of TCW">''[[The Art of Star Wars: The Clone Wars]]''</ref><ref name="Art of TCW 2">{{StoryCite|story=The Art of Star Wars: The Clone Wars (booklet)|book=Star Wars: The Clone Wars Seasons 1-5 Collector's Edition|bformatted=''Star Wars: The Clone Wars'' Seasons 1-5 Collector's Edition|sformatted=''The Art of Star Wars: The Clone Wars''|smanual=1}}</ref> Green-haired Narglatches were also originally meant to appear as mounts or beasts of burden for the [[Onderon rebels]] in the series' fifth season episode "[[Tipping Points]]". The creatures were designed and modeled, but were removed before the animation process as the series' supervising director [[Dave Filoni]] thought that the rebels would have more practical and less ferocious creatures.<ref name="Tipping EG">{{SW|url=series/clone-wars/tipping-points-episode-guide|text="Tipping Points" Episode Guide &#124; The Clone Wars}}</ref> A re-textured version of the narglatch model was designed for the unproduced '' The Clone Wars'' episode 6.13<ref name="UntoldTCWLiveblog">{{SW|url=news/swca-the-untold-clone-wars-panel-liveblog|text=SWCA: The Untold ''Clone Wars'' Panel Liveblog}}</ref> "Lethal Alliance".<ref name="DarkDiscipleEpisodes">{{Twitter|pablohidalgo|status/712366298148298752|[[Pablo Hidalgo]]|date=2016-03-22|quote=613-616, and then 705-708.}} identifies the production number of the first episode of the arc as "6.13" and ''[[Dark Disciple]]'' identifies the first episode as "Lethal Alliance".</ref> as part of a fountain. The model of the statues and the fountain was designed by [[Will Nichols]] under the title "Pantora City Marketplace Ext (Fountain)" and finished on the date "121003".<ref name="UntoldTCWLiveblog" /> The scene along with the rest of the arc was later adapted into the [[2015]] novel ''[[Dark Disciple]]''.<ref name="DD" />

